  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Anatomy Plate",
    "overview": "\n**Creative North Star: \"The Anatomy Plate\"**\n\nOne typewriter, drawn as a technical figure on a framed card, is the whole interface. The sheet rolled into its carriage is the test: the copy is printed faint on the paper, the learner types over it, and the machine answers each key with a stepped mechanical response. Everything outside the figure is set like a plate in a manual: a hairline frame, four corner captions, a legend under the card, and a single rail of controls above it that wears the same caption type. When the server returns the score, the same card turns to Fig 2, the result plate: the machine's figures, a hairline speed chart, a stats row and what to practise next.\n\nThe world is monochrome. The bench, card and machine are four-tone grey solids with 0.6px hairline outlines, screws drawn as dots, and ruler ticks cut to the type pitch. Colour exists only as ribbon ink: a black ribbon for correct letters and the red half of a bichrome ribbon for misses, on the paper and, lifted for contrast, on the result plate. The only lit part of the drawing is the strike-point mark. Dark (charcoal bench) and light (paper-white bench) are the same drawing with each tone remapped; the theme follows the system preference and has a toggle.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "One isometric anatomy figure owns the screen; the paper is the test surface.",
      "Four-tone monochrome solids, 0.6px hairlines, dots and ticks; no hue outside the ribbon's ink.",
      "One lit part: the strike-point mark.",
      "Courier Prime on the paper and for the machine's figures; small tracked system mono for every caption, control and axis.",
      "Square plate, near-square 2px chips, round 17px key discs, flat surfaces.",
      "Stepped motion; reduced motion holds the machine still and draws the chart at once."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Ink Is The Only Colour Rule",
        "body": "Hue appears only as ribbon ink: `ribbon-red` on paper, and `ribbon-red-plate` on the card, where it marks wrong keys and nothing else. The bench, card, machine, controls, captions and every other chart line stay on the grey ramp. The vendored kit's red, green and blue line tones are not part of this world.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Lit Part Rule",
        "body": "The strike-point mark is the only element painted with `lit`. No other solid in the figure is drawn lit, so the eye always finds the print point.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Paired Theme Rule",
        "body": "Every colour is declared twice, dark and light, on the same token name; a new tone without a light override is incomplete.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Pitch Cell Rule",
        "body": "Every character on paper sits centred in a fixed pitch cell (12.5 units at 21-unit type, 26 columns, wrapped at 23). The carriage scale carries one tick per cell. Proportional text never appears on the sheet.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Two Faces Rule",
        "body": "Courier Prime for paper and the machine's figures: the sheet, the copy holder, the countdown, the result figures, the stat values, the key discs and the typed missed words, always at 700 off the paper. System mono for everything else: captions, controls, the readout, axis labels, notes. No third family is shown; the sans stack on the page root is a fallback that no visible text uses.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Figures Scale With The Card Rule",
        "body": "The countdown and the result figures size in container units of the card (`cqw`), not the window, so they keep their proportion to the drawing.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Drawn Depth Rule",
        "body": "Volume belongs to the figure. Interface surfaces stay flat and outlined; the lift stack is reserved for the one transient prompt over the figure.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do put every reading surface of the test on paper inside the drawing, set in Courier Prime on the pitch grid; put results on Fig 2 inside the same card.",
      "Do set controls as chips in the rail, using label type and the chip's ring states; show binary tools with `aria-pressed`.",
      "Do declare every new colour as a dark and light pair on the same token name.",
      "Do keep machine motion stepped: one cell per key, two-frame strikes, glides only for the return and the line feed. Result motion is one pen trace and one fade. Nothing moves under reduced motion.",
      "Do keep the plate's captions to the four corners: figure number, object and test, hint, status readout.",
      "Do keep chart marks off the wpm gridlines by choosing the error axis top, not by nudging marks."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't use hue anywhere but ribbon ink: `ribbon-red` on the sheet, `ribbon-red-plate` for wrong keys on the card. The kit's red, green and blue line tones stay out.",
      "Don't paint a second part of the figure with `lit`; the strike point is the only lit part.",
      "Don't add drop shadows to cards, chips, discs or the tip; depth belongs to the drawing.",
      "Don't set proportional or sans text on the paper, or Courier Prime anywhere but the paper and the machine's figures; captions, controls and axis labels stay in system mono.",
      "Don't show live wpm or accuracy during the test; the countdown is the only live figure.",
      "Don't use the kit's `muted` grey (#6e6e6e on the card) for text; this build raises captions and legend to `text` for contrast."
    ]
  }
}
